### Background and motivation

Today we have `X509FindType.FindByThumbprint` which implicitly uses the SHA-1 algorithm. Regardless of the context and nature of security, SHA-1 is being phased out and the use of it ranges from discouraged to forbidden when writing net-new code.

To help with the transition away from SHA-1, we should offer finding certificates in a certificate collection and certificate store by their SHA-2-256 thumbprint (or any other hash).

### API Proposal

```C#
namespace System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;

public partial class X509Certificate2Collection {
    public X509Certificate2Collection FindByThumbprint(HashAlgorithmName hashAlgorithm, string thumbprintHex);
    public X509Certificate2Collection FindByThumbprint(HashAlgorithmName hashAlgorithm, ReadOnlySpan<char> thumbprintHex);
    public X509Certificate2Collection FindByThumbprint(HashAlgorithmName hashAlgorithm, ReadOnlySpan<byte> thumbprint);
}
```


### API Usage

```csharp
// maybe this comes from config.
string sha256Thumbprint = "000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f101112131415161718191a1b1c1d1e1f";
using var store = new X509Store(StoreName.My, StoreLocation.CurrentUser);
store.Open(OpenFlags.ReadOnly);
X509Certificate2Collection found = store.Certificates.FindByThumbprint(HashAlgorithmName.SHA256, sha256Thumbprint);

// found will contain certificate with matching thumbprint
```


### Alternative Designs

If we do not want a new API on `X509Certificate2Collection`, then we can add a new enum member to `X509FindType` with the name `ThumbprintSha256`. The upshot to this is it extends the existing `Find` API. The downside to this is does not generalize it to support any `HashAlgorithmName`. Generalizing to support the same inputs as `X509Certificate.GetCertHashString` seems like a better idea.

